From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bert Belder <bertbelder@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: Route pointer tables through the IsolateGroup page allocator

An isolate's pointer tables (external pointer, cpp heap pointer,
trusted pointer and JS dispatch tables, plus the shared external and
trusted pointer tables it owns) are out-of-sandbox memory like the
code range and trusted range, but each table reserves its backing as
a subspace of the process-wide platform virtual address space. An
embedder that supplies v8::IsolateGroup::CreateParams::page_allocator
to place a group's out-of-sandbox memory therefore still finds the
tables at addresses V8 picks.

Let SegmentedTableBase::Initialize() take an optional embedder page
allocator. When given, the table's reservation is taken from it (as
inaccessible pages, at the table's alignment) and managed by a small
VirtualAddressSpace over that reservation, which hands out and frees
the table's segments through the allocator's SetPermissions and
DecommitPages. It applies a memory protection key to the reservation
and to decommitted pages as VirtualAddressSubspace does, so
write-protected tables keep working with PKU-based thread isolation.
Isolate::Init() passes the group's embedder allocator to every table
it initializes; with none supplied nothing changes.

